# Livelit Hygiene #

# A Macro livelit's use means code it generates, applied to code the #
# client wrote -- the splices, edited inside the widget. Two programs #
# are being combined, and each has its own names. Hygiene is the #
# promise that neither can reach into the other by accident. #

# The paper calls it the binding discipline (Sec. 2.4.3) and asks for #
# two things; generating an expansion, as Dynamic Row or Column does, #
# asks for a third. Each has a slide of its own in this folder, with #
# what naive textual expansion -- pasting the client's code into the #
# generated code -- would do instead: #

# 1. Capture Avoidance: the client's x is not the expansion's x. #
#    The use means 11; naive expansion gives 20. #
# 2. Context Independence: the expansion's names are not the #
#    client's. The use means 3; naive expansion gives 0. #
# 3. Generated Binders: names the author picks can collide. #
#    With Lambda the use means [2, 2], where [1, 2] was meant. #
# 4. Abs: binders the system names cannot. The use means [1, 2]. #

# Slide 3 is the deck's one unhygienic example, there to show the #
# capture. Every other expansion, Dynamic Row or Column's included, #
# binds with Abs or inside a quotation. #

# Why it holds: a splice reaches the expansion only as an ARGUMENT, #
# and application substitutes without capture; the expansion is #
# checked and run in the builtin context, so it is closed; and Abs #
# names each generated binder with a name no program can write. #

"see the four slides that follow"
